On May 25, 10:08 pm, Meddyan <Medd...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Yesterday I was playing a few wizards, and was thinking about what the
> best possible start-up inventory could be. Heres what I would go for
> if I could control the RNG (assuming a magic marker and blind fold as
> well):

My list:

scrolls: genocide, plus any two of enchant armour, enchant weapon,
charging, and stinking cloud.
With genocide and a marker, the first cursed scroll of blank paper has
a good chance of becoming SDSM.  The others, except for stinking
cloud, I think are pretty obvious.  Stinking cloud is a nice when
"kill something extra nasty" or "crowd control" is needed.

spellbook: magic missile, detect monsters, foo-healing, light,
protection, clairvoyance, jumping, and haste-self.
All of these are useful from either an offensive, defensive, or
avoidance standpoint.

potions: b!see invisible, sickness and foo-healing
Intrinsic see invisible is very nice in the early game.  I rarely have
the intrinsic by the time stalkers start showing up, although an elf
with a wand of make invisible can also be nasty.  Sickness plus a
stack of darts, do the maths. :)  The foo-healing should be obvious.

rings: Any two of free action, conflict, teleportation, see invisible,
invisibility, and poison resistance.
As Mike said elsethread, free action isn't immediately useful.  It's
also the one ring I WILL NOT do the endgame without.  Conflict thins
the crowd.  Teleportation lets one run away.  see invisible and
invisibility provide extrinsic sources of two intrinsics that usually
take awhile to get.  Poison resistance puts kobolds and other
poisonous stuff on the menu from turn 1 as well as eliminating
poisoned weapons/spikes as an insta-kill concern.

wand: speed monster, death, teleportation, or fire.
Speed monster makes monster avoidance easier.  The other three provide
a limited-use "make it go away" services.

